AI Task 2 – Implementing Parallelism with PyTorch

Prompt:
How can I implement the Sierpiński Triangle using PyTorch tensors so that many points are processed together instead of using a Python loop for every individual point?

Result:
The AI suggested storing all points in a single PyTorch tensor, generating random vertex selections for all points at once, and updating their coordinates using:

points = (points + selected_vertices) / 2

This allows the same transformation to be applied to many points simultaneously using vectorised tensor operations.

Technical Challenge:
The most difficult part was understanding how PyTorch was being used as a major part of the fractal algorithm rather than only for storing data. I learned that the tensor operations update many points together, which provides parallelism and avoids processing every point individually with a Python loop.

Outcome:
I tested the implementation, confirmed that it generated the expected Sierpiński Triangle, and verified that the main fractal calculations were performed using PyTorch tensors.